  "Question": "Al’s age is \\(16\\) more than the sum of Bob’s age and Carl’s age, and the square of Al’s age is \\(1632\\) more than the square of the sum of Bob’s age and Carl’s age. The sum of the ages of Al, Bob and Carl is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(64\\)",
    "B": "\\(94\\)",
    "C": "\\(96\\)",
    "D": "\\(102\\)",
    "E": "\\(140\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Bob's and Carl's ages appear only through their sum, so let \\(s\\) denote their combined age. Al's age is then \\(s+16\\), and the requested total is \\(2s+16\\).\n\nThe difference-of-squares identity is useful because its sum factor is exactly this total. The second condition gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n1632&=(s+16)^2-s^2\\\\\n&=((s+16)-s)((s+16)+s)\\\\\n&=16(2s+16).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nDividing by \\(16\\), we obtain the total directly:\n\\[\n2s+16=\\frac{1632}{16}=102.\n\\]\nThis corresponds to \\(s=43\\) and Al's age \\(59\\), consistent with the given age difference. The sum is \\(102\\) years, choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
